research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-a6-repair-fs-nth-term-test-converse-final.log-8761-research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-708044-+{"id":"cor-raabe-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state Raabe's positive-term test and identify it as Kummer with harmonic weights. The item uses k+1 rather than k because its naturals start at zero, and uses liminf/limsup rather than assuming a single limit. The proof is the sourced Kummer specialization with that convention delta.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-a6-repair-fs-nth-term-test-converse-final.log-8762-research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-708045-+{"id":"thm-gauss-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests"],"rationale":"The sources state Gauss's test for a_n/a_{n+1}=1+alpha/n+O(n^{-beta}) with beta>1, convergent exactly for alpha>1. The item's bound |r_k|<=C k^{-1-epsilon}, epsilon positive rational, is the same big-O regime after choosing beta=1+epsilon, with local rational-power conventions. Its borderline proof is a locally constructed summable-error product argument rather than the sourced logarithmic/Kummer proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-a6-repair-fs-nth-term-test-converse-final.log:8763:research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-708046-+{"id":"thm-abel-dini","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The source states the divergent-series Abel–Dini–Pringsheim theorem for positive terms with inclusive partial sums S_n: sum a_n/S_n diverges and sum a_n/S_n^t converges for t>1, hence in particular at t=2. These are exactly the item's two claims and convention. The local proof derives the two estimates directly from Cauchy and telescoping facts.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/certifier-a6-repair-fs-nth-term-test-converse-final.log:8764:research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-708047-+{"id":"rem-strength-order-of-the-nonnegative-tests","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html","https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The source set establishes the ratio-root strength order, Kummer specializations, Raabe and Gauss criteria, and the Abel–Dini no-final-comparison obstruction. The item is a repository-specific synthesis of those results and local availability constraints. It also contains one inaccurate alteration about a 1/(k log k) Gauss remainder, recorded as an A2 falsehood; as a Remark it has no separate proof component.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
