research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699366-+$1/(k\log k)$ is not absolutely summable, and a term of that order changes the
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699367-+Bertrand borderline. In the model
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699368-+$a_k/a_{k+1}=1+1/k+c/(k\log k)+o(1/(k\log k))$, the value of $c$ distinguishes
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:699369:+convergent from divergent logarithmically corrected harmonic series. It is not
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699370-+a harmless Gauss remainder. Thomson–Bruckner–Bruckner Theorem 3.41 and the
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699371-+standard convergence-test statement instead use an error
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699372-+$O(k^{-\beta})$ for some $\beta>1$.
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699385-+exponent restriction does not cost generality within the classical
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-699386-+$O(k^{-\beta})$, $\beta>1$, hypothesis.
