research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761150-+$1/(k\log k)$ is not absolutely summable, and a term of that order changes the
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761151-+Bertrand borderline. In the model
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761152-+$a_k/a_{k+1}=1+1/k+c/(k\log k)+o(1/(k\log k))$, the value of $c$ distinguishes
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:761153:+convergent from divergent logarithmically corrected harmonic series. It is not
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761154-+a harmless Gauss remainder. Thomson–Bruckner–Bruckner Theorem 3.41 and the
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761155-+standard convergence-test statement instead use an error
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761156-+$O(k^{-\beta})$ for some $\beta>1$.
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761169-+exponent restriction does not cost generality within the classical
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761170-+$O(k^{-\beta})$, $\beta>1$, hypothesis.
