research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761179-+> divergent series in the family.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761180-+
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761181-+The two displayed series do not license the last clause. In the usual fixed
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:761182:+family $\sum 1/(kH_k^t)$, the Abel–Dini–Pringsheim threshold is divergence for
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761183-+$t\le 1$ and convergence for $t>1$, so $t=1$ is precisely the divergent
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761184-+endpoint in the exponent order. The true no-slowest-series conclusion comes
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761185-+from applying the construction again to the newly obtained divergent series.
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761188-+
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761189-+> So exponent $1$ gives a divergent member and exponent $2$ a convergent one.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761190-+> The absence of a slowest divergent positive series comes from applying
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log:761191:+> Abel–Dini again to the newly produced divergent series, not from a
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-761192-+> last-exponent claim about this fixed pair.
