$\sum_{k \ge 1} 1/(k H_k^{2})$, with $H_k = 1 + 1/2 + \dots + 1/k$.

**What the pair shows.** The harmonic series [-already diverges as-]{+is a familiar+} slowly [-as any-]{+divergent+}
explicit [-series on this page,-]{+series,+} and dividing its terms by the running total
produces something that diverges more slowly still. Dividing by the square of the
running total overshoots into convergence. So [-the two exponents-]{+exponent+} $1$ {+gives a divergent+}
{+member+} and {+exponent+} $2$ [-straddle the boundary, and there is no last-]{+a convergent one. The absence of a slowest+} divergent
